  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nUpper level ridging continues over the region today, bringing \nmostly sunny skies, dry conditions and above average temperatures.\nAs this afternoon progresses, the ridge will begin to flatten, \nincreasing the pressure gradient and creating windy conditions, \nmostly confined to the Rocky Mountain Front. The ridge continues \nto flatten into Saturday, causing the pressure gradient to tighten\neven more as convergence aloft increases. This will bring strong \nupper level winds that, combined with diurnal heating and terrain \ninfluences, will mix down to the surface along the northern Rocky \nMountain Front. This will bring very windy conditions to the \nnorthern Rocky Mountain Front, specifically in the area north of \nHighway 200 and west of I-15. The gusty winds are expected to \ndissipate by late Saturday evening as the strongest upper level \nwinds move east and diurnal heating subsides.\n\nZonal flow aloft will build again on Sunday, allowing for \nslightly cooler temperatures and continuing the stretch of dry \nweather. By Monday morning, weak ridging takes its place as an \nupper level trough begins to move down the Pacific Coast towards \nnorthern California. This will create southwesterly flow aloft by \nMonday evening, which will last through the middle of next week, \nallowing for seasonably average temperatures. \n\nBy Thursday, the trough will have moved south of the Great Basin,\ncreating a more direct southerly flow aloft, before moving \nnortheast towards Wyoming. This will allow temperatures to warm on\nThursday before cooling down on Friday. Increased moisture aloft \nwill get carried north by the upper level southerly low, \nincreasing precipitation chances towards the end of next week into\nnext weekend. There remains shearing and splitting in the trough \nlate Thursday into next weekend, so while one can expect cooling \ntemperatures and increased precipitation chances late in the \nforecast period, confidence in the forecast specifics for this \ntime remains low.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nConfidence has increased on stronger winds impacting the northern\nRocky Mountain Front Saturday. Models are in good agreement on an\nincrease in the 700 mb winds, now showing around 75-80 kts, and \nvertical cross sections show these strong winds mixing down to the\nsurface from late Saturday morning to late Saturday night due to \ndiurnal heating and turbulent mixing from the terrain of the Rocky\nMountains. As a result, there is also more model agreement on the\nwind gusts at the surface, with the spread of 10th percentile and\n90th percentile wind gusts only being about a 10 mph difference. \nDue to the stronger 700 mb winds and increased confidence in their\ntiming and location, the High Wind Watch (along the northern \nRocky Mountain Front) has now been upgraded to a High Wind \nWarning, with gusts up to 85 mph possible. \n\nConfidence was lower on these stronger 700 mb winds mixing to the\nsurface along the rest of the Hi-Line. Soundings showed a low \nlevel inversion across the Hi-Line, which would inhibit mixing. \nThis is supported by the vertical cross sections showing the \nstronger winds aloft not mixing down to the surface. While windy \nconditions will still be present across the Hi-Line tomorrow, the \nlower confidence in the strongest winds being mixed down to the \nsurface resulted in the High Wind Watch not being extended across \nthe Hi-Line. -Dzomba",
